{"kind":"report","text":"Approved exact head bfcd6683bff87a993917aff027d77017a50fbcef, both artifacts. No blocking findings.\n\nArchitecture: layer 5 receipt admission and layer 6 cleanup accounting preserve docs/reference/architecture.md at this head. A receipt still captures protection once at its incoming frontier. Only the staleness argument to projectCommitments is removed for this membership question. Source inspection confirms staleness does not choose rows or their request/promise/report identities: it changes flags and turns an uncompleted open/promised row into stale, all within the unsettled population. completionStatus and landing accounting do not read those flags. No profile, persisted representation or architecture contract changes.\n\nSecurity: validateMergeReceiptNow remains the source of authority; the capture still precedes addDecision, so a receipt cannot settle its own protector before protection is judged. A completed or withdrawn commitment cannot become protection merely because its basis is stale. Current sibling cleanup accounting recomputes the set after later acts. Actor signatures, review independence and merge retirement scope are unchanged. No unresolved trust or authority defect found.\n\nSimplification and cost: this is smaller than a maintained incremental index, with no invalidation state. Before, each receipt paid whole-graph staleness S(N) plus commitment projection C(N); now it pays C(N). R receipts still cost the sum of their commitment projections and scan costs. This is not constant-time capture and the report says so. The request's outcome and five conditions concern removing the dominant recomputation while preserving the answer; this change meets that outcome without claiming its title's stronger cost independence. No further index or broad rewrite is needed to approve this improvement.\n\nIndependent exact-head checks: the complete workroom package passed with race checking, count=1 (10.503 s); go vet and git diff --check